  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 100 AM CDT Sun Oct 4 2026\n\nRadar currently shows showers hastily departing the forecast area\nearly this morning as LLJ ejects to the northeast and rain outruns\nbetter forcing along the front. Showers should completely exit by\naround 08Z, though remnant moisture under shallow inversion should\nkeep some low clouds around through most of the morning. Skies\nshould then clear out this afternoon as winds shift to the\nnorthwest and cooler, more stable air filters in.\n\nFrost/freeze potential... Par for the course, frost potential on\ntrack for late tonight into Monday morning as high sits overhead,\nwinds decrease, and windows for clearing occur. MOS guidance once\nagain showing solid signal for temperatures in the low to mid 30s\nover portions of north-central to far northeast Wisconsin, with \na couple cold spots along the Upper Peninsula border even \ndropping below freezing. Have opted to throw out a Frost Advisory \nfor areas north of Hwy 29 where confidence is greatest (60 to 90%)\nfor temperatures below 36 degrees. Similar to previous nights,\npossibility exists for an upgrade to Freeze Warning for a county\nor two, though forecast lows below freezing were not widespread\nenough to do so on this shift. Would also not be surprised to see\nsome shallow ground fog around early Monday morning due to high\nRHs. Frost threat decreasing for Monday night as gradient winds\nincrease and mid clouds stick around.\n\nDry pattern ahead... Ridge of high pressure will be the main \ndriver of the synoptic pattern through the extended, with \nensembles continuing to move the mid-week cold front through our\narea mostly dry. Beyond that, not seeing any strong signals for \norganized systems through at least the middle of the month. With \nfrost/freeze headlines already in play, this may result in \nincreasing fire weather concerns over the next several weeks due \nto current drought status and increasing fine fuels.",
